<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arab Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Arab Revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arab Revolt was an armed uprising by the Hashemite-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-eastern-theatre-of-world-war-i">Middle Eastern theatre of World War I</a>. On the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-hussein-correspondence">McMahon–Hussein Correspondence</a>, Hussein bin Ali launched it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> on 10 June 1916, aiming at an independent, unified Arab state from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had promised to recognise. With British backing and officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a>, the Sharifian Army drove the Ottomans from much of the Hejaz and Transjordan, and by 1918 had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and proclaimed the Arab Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> under Faisal I. Most Arabs in the Ottoman territories did not join it, and its forces played a limited supporting role.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arab-revolt.jpg" alt="Arab Revolt" width="330" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Bauche (1878–1956) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:030Arab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the First World War the <b>Arab Revolt</b> against the Ottoman Empire broke out in 1916, and British forces drove the Ottomans out of the Levant in 1917–1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a tribal army inspired by Wahhabism and led by Faisal Al-Dawish, he took Al-Ahsa from the Ottomans in 1913, stayed out of the <b>Arab Revolt</b> of 1916 that made Hussein bin Ali King of Hejaz, defeated the Al Rashid and took the title Sultan of Nejd in 1921.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He had served with the <b>Arab Revolt</b> and wrote Seven Pillars of Wisdom.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
